What Should I Do So I Can Work?
Pass a Japanese language test and a skills proficiency test. (People who have satisfactorily completed Technical Intern Training do not need to take any tests.)
Sign a contract with a company.
Note: This applies if you will be working in a field related to the job for which you received Technical Intern Training.
If you sign a contract with a company, you can get a detailed explanation of your contract and job before you submit your status of residence application.
Picking you up when you arrive in Japan and dropping you off when you leave Japan to return to your home country
Additionally, when you return to your home country, someone from the company will accompany you as far as the security check at the airport.
Helping you prepare the house you will live in and sign contracts for electricity and gas
Additionally, the company will assist you with setting up a bank account and signing contracts for your cell phone, electricity, and gas.
Additionally, they will teach you how to ride buses and trains and what to do if an earthquake occurs.
Accompanying you to city hall and other offices for various procedures
Additionally, they will help you fill out the paperwork needed for those procedures.
They will provide advice and respond to questions in your native language whenever a problem arises.
Help you communicate with Japanese people
The company will help you look for your next job if they intend to lay you off.
If you feel that you haven’t received proper support from your company, please contact the nearest Immigration office.
