News - Playing a new movement of Poverty reduction in Zimbabwe’s livestock industry —- Wu Chunhua, deputy leader of China’s third high-level Agricultural expert Group for Zimbabwe
Playing a new movement of Poverty reduction in Zimbabwe’s livestock industry —- Wu Chunhua, deputy leader of China’s third high-level Agricultural expert Group for Zimbabwe
On 28 January 2019, a group of experts visited the Poultry station of the Henderson Institute to help their local counterparts launch poultry hatches. At the poultry station, Wu saw two china-assisted hatchers from 2016, but no matching hatchers.
“If there is no running water supply, then these fully automatic hatching devices cannot be used, which may be one of the reasons it has not been activated.” Wu chunhua said. After full research and demonstration, he designed the model of water-storage water supply and moisturizing system, which can better solve the problem of automatic water supply of equipment.
In the China-Zimbabwe agricultural cooperation demonstration village centered on Yemenba village in Cheriman, the indigenous chicken breeding project is the first poultry assistance project carried out by the expert group. The project, with a total investment of $14,000, was launched on March 8, 2020, and a total of 14 farmers from the village, including Andrew, applied to participate.
Referring to the architectural drawings designed by Wu chunhua, the team mobilized 14 farmers to build chicken houses in their courtyards. Each chicken house has four feeding rooms and a warehouse, with a single building area of 20 square meters.
“This pilot and demonstration project can provide a reference for the breeding of local chickens in Zimbabwe.” The breed must have both the survival adaptability and unique food flavor of native chickens and the high-yield performance of exotic chickens, Wu said. If successful, the project will benefit the largest number of Zimbabweans.
According to Wu, the first set of incubation equipment was put into trial operation on November 28, 2020, with an annual capacity of 50,000 eggs, and the capacity will be doubled after another set of equipment is upgraded. “This is the first time that a Chinese incubator has been put into use in Tianjin.
According to Wu, the original plan was to finish brooding in six weeks, but due to the epidemic, the brooding was completed on March 8 this year, and the chicks were 80 days old. 184 chicks were used in the experiment and demonstration of house feeding and free-range raising of native chickens, and the rest were distributed to farmers for raising.
You use your intelligence and wisdom to serve our poverty reduction wholeheartedly, without eating a meal from us or drinking a mouthful of our water. You are a messenger from God, and we thank you!” Andrew thanked him.
In the eyes of Wu Chunhua, giving money and material is not as good as giving technology, not to mention updating ideas. Therefore, he made the knowledge of animal husbandry better understood and learned through training.
Now, Wu’s hope is that the experience of building poultry hatches and brood houses in the demonstration villages will be replicated in Zimbabwe, helping other institutions, including the Henderson Institute and Chibero College of Agriculture, build more practical poultry hatches and brood houses. To do some useful work for the captive incubation and brood of poultry in Zimbabwe.
